<section xml:id="section-fields-definition">
  <title>Definitions Concerning Fields</title>
  <p>
    I defined a field way back in <xref ref="definition-rings-types"/>
    and we talked about fields all through the ring theory section of
    these notes. Therefore, I'm not starting from scratch here. We
    already know a fair bit about fields: everything is invertible,
    there are no interesting ideals, we can make fraction fields from
    domains, and so on. Field homomorphisms are just ring
    homomorphisms, so we already know about homomorphisms as well. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I also can draw some conclusion based on what we already know. For
    example, the fact that ideals are not interesting is quite useful.
    Here are the most important implications.
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>K</m> and <m>L</m> be a fields. The following facts are
        true. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            There are no field quotients of <m>K</m> over than
            <m>K</m> itself or the zero ring. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Let <m>\phi: K \rightarrow L</m> be a homomorphism. Then
            either <m>\phi</m> is the zero map (sending everything in
            <m>K</m> to <m>0 \in L</m>), or <m>\phi</m> is injective. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The notions of prime ideals and factoring don't apply in
            fields. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        All of these follow, more or less directly, from the fact that
        fields don't have non-trivial ideals. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    The fact that all field maps are injections leads to the following
    definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: K \rightarrow L</m> be a field map which is not
        the zero map (and therefore is injective). Then this map is
        called a <term>field extension</term> or, alternatively,
        <m>L</m> is called a field extension of <m>K</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    This definition really indicates the direction of field theory. We
    start will relatively small fields and look at how their
    extensions work. We already know have a good source of extension
    from ring theory. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>K</m> be a field and let <m>p(x) \in K[x]</m> be an
        irreducible polynomial. Then the ring quotient <m>K[x]/\langle
        p(x) \rangle</m> is a field extension of <m>K</m>. Moreover,
        this is equivalent to attaching a new element <m>\alpha</m> to
        <m>K</m> where <m>\alpha</m> is a root of the polynomial. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        More of this is already from ring theory, where we understand
        this quotient ring. I'll leave the rest for you to figure out
        if you wish (like the fact that this is a field). 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    The notation here is a little different from the extensions of the
    integers. <m>K(\alpha)</m> is the extenion made by attaching the
    root of an irreducible polynomial to a field. If the irreducible
    polynomial had degree <m>d</m>, then elements of <m>K(\alpha)</m>
    can be written as follows for <m>c_i \in K</m>. 
    <me>
      c_0 + c_1 \alpha + c_2 \alpha^2 + \ldots + c_{d-1} \alpha^{d-1}
    </me>
    This form is justified by the replacement of <m>\alpha^d</m> using
    the polynomial. For example, if the polynomial <m>x^2 + x + 8</m>
    is irreducible, then a root satisfies <m>\alpha^2 + \alpha + 8 =
    0</m> which is equivalent to <m>\alpha^2 = - \alpha - 8</m>. This
    form lets me replace <m>\alpha^2</m> whenever I find it. 
  </p>
  <p>
    All this leads into the discussion of vector spaces. In <xref
    ref="definition-vector-space-informal"/>, I gave an informal
    definition of a vector space. Now that we have the language of
    abstract algebra, let me give a formal version.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>K</m> be a field. A <term>vector space</term> over
        <m>K</m> is a set <m>V</m> with the following properties. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>V</m> is an abelian group. 
          </li>
          <li>
            For all <m>k \in K</m> and <m>v \in V</m>, there is an
            operation called scalar multiplication for which <m> kv
            \in V</m>. This operation has three properties. 
            <md>
              <mrow>
                k(v + u) = kv + ku 
              </mrow>
              <mrow>
                (k + l) v = kv + lv 
              </mrow>
              <mrow>
                (kl) v = k(lv) 
              </mrow>
            </md>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    A vector space is an object of abstract algebra: it is an abelian
    group with scalar multiplication by a field. I left the
    <sq>scalars</sq> vague earlier, but here they are specific. The
    scalars must belong to a field. From this definition, all of the
    tools of linear algebra will apply: linear combinations,
    dimension, basis, linear transformations, linear subspaces, and so
    on. It just now happens over an arbitrary field. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Vector spaces help me understand field extensions. Here is an
    important proposition.
  </p>
  <proposition xml:id="prop-field-extension-vector-space">
    <statement>
      <p>
        All field extensions are vector spaces over the smaller field. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        This is pretty easy to check. The scalar multiplication comes
        just from the field arithmetic, since the smaller field is
        (isomorphic to) a subset of the larger field. The rules are
        all from the basic ring theory of a field. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    With this, I can start to use the language of vector spaces to
    understand field extensions. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\phi: K \rightarrow L</m> be a field extension. The
        dimension of <m>L</m> as a <m>K</m>-vector space is called the
        <term>degree</term> of the field extension an written
        <m>[L:K]</m>. Here are some related terms. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            A degree 2 extension is a <term>quadratic
            extension</term>.
          </li>
          <li>
            A degree 3 extension is a <term>cubic
            extension</term>.
          </li>
          <li>
            Higher degree extensions are numbered likewise. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A finite degree extension is a <term>finite
            extension</term>. Otherwise, it is an <term>infinite
            extension</term>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Let me think about <m>K(\alpha)</m> again using this language. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>K(a)</m> be a field extension formed by attaching one
        new element to the field. This kind of extension is called a
        <term>simple extension</term>. If the extension is finite,
        then <m>\alpha</m> must be the root of some irreducible
        polynomial <m>p(x)</m> and the extension must be isomorphic to
        <m>K[x]/\langle p(x) \rangle</m>. In this case, the degree of
        the extension is equal to the degree of the polynomial. If the
        extension is infinite, then <m>\alpha</m> cannot be the root
        of any polynomial over <m>K</m>. Such an <m>\alpha</m> is
        called a <term>transcendental element</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    This gives us a lot of information. Let me not prove but at least
    explain the dimension statement. Again, consider <m>K[x]/\langle p(x)
    \rangle</m>. As before, the form of elements is as follows, where
    <m>d</m> is the degree of the polynomial. 
    <me>
      c_0 + c_1 \alpha + c_2 \alpha^2 + \ldots + c_{d-1} \alpha^{d-1}
    </me>
    In the vector space, I just care about the coefficients. These
    coefficients are equivalent to a vector.
    <me>
      (c_0, c_1, c_2, \ldots, c_{d-1}) 
    </me>
    The set of all vectors like this is a vector space of dimension
    <m>d</m>, justifying the degree claim for the extension. With this
    perspective, here is a basis for the field <m>K(\alpha)</m> as a
    vector space. 
    <me>
      \{1, \alpha, \alpha^2, \ldots, \alpha^{d-1} \}
    </me>
    There are <m>d</m> elements in this basis, again matching the
    degree <m>d</m> of the extension.
  </p>
  <p>
    What do the elements of a transcnendental extension look like?
    Well, let <m>K(\alpha)</m> be a transcendental extension. Since
    <m>\alpha</m> is not the root of any polynomial, no powers of it
    can be replaced. If <m>c \in K</m>, then I can consider element
    like <m>c\alpha^i</m> for <m>i \in \ZZ</m> (including negative
    exponents). All of these are distinct. These form a basis for this
    infinite vector space. 
    <me>
      \left\{ \ldots \frac{1}{\alpha^3}, \frac{1}{\alpha^2}
      \frac{1}{\alpha}, 1 , \alpha, \alpha^2, \alpha^3, \ldots
      \right\}
    </me>
    This is an infinite basis. I haven't talked about infinite vector
    spaces that must so far, but let me state one important notion
    here: the basis might be infinite, but the elements of the vector
    space are <em>finite</em> sums taken from this infinite basis.
    Therefore, elements of the transcendental extension look like this
    example. 
    <me>
      c_{-7} \frac{1}{\alpha^7} + c_{-2} \frac{1}{\alpha^2} + c_0 +
      c_2 \alpha^2 + c_{105} \alpha^{105}
    </me>
    However, infinite sums are not elements of this vector space, not
    even if they would otherwise converge. 
    <me>
      \sum_{n=0}^\infty c_n \alpha^n \notin K(\alpha) 
    </me>
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me focus on extensions of <m>\QQ</m> for some examples. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\QQ(\sqrt{2})</m> is a quadratic extenion of <m>\QQ</m>.
      Element have the forn <m>a + b \sqrt{2}</m> with rational
      coefficients. The inverse of a non-zero element is defined as
      follows. 
      <me>
        (a + b \sqrt{2})^{-1} = \frac{a - b \sqrt{2}}{a^2 + 2b^2} 
      </me>
      Feel free to check that this is, in fact, a multiplicative
      inverse. The ring <m>\ZZ[\sqrt{2}]</m> is a subring of this
      field. (This field is, indeed, the field of fractions of this
      subring.) 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\QQ(\sqrt[3]{7})</m> is a cubic extension of <m>\QQ</m>.
      Elements have the form <m>a + b\sqrt[3]{7} + c \sqrt[3]{49}</m>.
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\QQ(i)</m> is a quadratic extension of <m>\QQ</m>.
      <m>\ZZ[i]</m> is a subring and, again, this field is the field
      of fractions of the subring. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\QQ(\pi)</m> is a transcendental infinite extension of
      <m>\QQ</m>. Elements are finite sums of integer powers of
      <m>\pi</m> with rational coefficient. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    All of these field extensions sit between <m>\QQ</m> and
    <m>\CC</m>. The study of fields <m>K</m> such that <m>\QQ \subset K
    \subset \CC</m> is, in fact, a major piece of field theory in
    mathematics. Let me finish this section with two definitions. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>K</m> be a finite extension of <m>\QQ</m>. Then
        <m>K</m> is called a <term>number field</term>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\overline{\QQ}</m> consist of the field generated by
        all elements which are algebraic over <m>\QQ</m>: that is, all
        roots of any possible polynomials over <m>\QQ</m>. This is an
        infinite extension of <m>\QQ</m>, but still a proper subset of
        <m>\CC</m>, since it does not contain any transcendental
        elements. <m>\overline{Q}</m> is called the <term>algebraic
        closure</term> of <m>\QQ</m>. This term can be generalized to any
        field: its algebraic closure is the field which contains all
        elements which are algebraic over the field (and the sums of
        all such elements). 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
</section>
